+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — sqlparser 0.62.0
+
+Tests were added with `hegeltest` 0.28.2 following the hegel skill workflow.
+Locations:
+
+- `tests/sqlparser_common.rs` — `mod hegel_properties` (9 tests, including one
+  stateful model test)
+- `src/tokenizer.rs` — `mod tests::hegel_properties` (4 tests)
+
+## Properties tested
+
+| # | Property | Test | Evidence |
+|---|----------|------|----------|
+| 1 | `Parser::parse_sql` never panics on arbitrary input, for all 16 dialects | `hegel_parse_never_panics_on_arbitrary_input` (tests/sqlparser_common.rs) | The crate's own fuzz target `fuzz/fuzz_targets/fuzz_parse_sql.rs` checks exactly this; `parse_sql` returns `Result`. Inputs are arbitrary text plus "SQL soup" (SQL fragments glued together) to reach deeper parser states. |
+| 2 | Display/reparse round-trip: for a parseable statement, `parse(stmt.to_string()) == [stmt]` | `hegel_parse_display_reparse_is_identity` | `src/lib.rs` "Creating SQL text from AST" ("The original SQL text can be generated from the AST"), and `one_statement_parses_to`/`verified_stmt` in `src/test_utils.rs`, which assert this contract for hand-picked examples. Statements are generated by a grammar-based composite generator (SELECT with subqueries/WHERE/ORDER BY/LIMIT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE; expressions with binary/unary ops, idents, string and integer literals). |
+| 3 | Pretty-printed SQL (`{:#}`) reparses to the same AST as the compact form | `hegel_pretty_print_reparses_to_same_ast` | `src/lib.rs` "Pretty Printing" section; `tests/pretty_print.rs` presents pretty output as real SQL. |
+| 4 | Integer literal text is preserved verbatim through parsing, for the full `u128` range | `hegel_number_literal_roundtrip` | `Value::Number(String, bool)` stores the literal text verbatim (src/ast/value.rs); no numeric conversion is documented. |
+| 5 | Quoted identifiers (`"` on Generic, `` ` `` on MySQL) round-trip through Display + parse, over the domain `EscapeQuotedString` documents as round-trippable | `hegel_quoted_identifier_roundtrip` | `Ident::with_quote` docs, `impl Display for Ident` uses `escape_quoted_string`, `parse_quoted_ident` folds doubled quotes (src/tokenizer.rs:2364). Generator boosts values containing the quote char (boundary-conjunction guidance from SKILL.md). |
+| 6 | Bracket-quoted identifiers (`[`, MSSQL style) round-trip through Display + parse | `hegel_bracket_quoted_identifier_roundtrip` | Same round-trip contract; `Ident::with_quote` lists `[` as valid. **FAILS — suspected real bug, kept failing (see below).** |
+| 7 | Single-quoted string literal content round-trips through Display + parse (GenericDialect), over the documented round-trippable domain | `hegel_string_literal_roundtrip` | `escape_single_quote_string` doubles `'` on Display; the tokenizer folds `''`. Generator boosts values containing `'`. |
+| 8 | Parsing `k` statements joined by `"; "` equals the concatenation of parsing each individually | `hegel_statement_sequences_parse_individually` | `Parser::parse_sql` handles multi-statement input; `statements_parse_to` in `src/test_utils.rs` joins canonical statements with `"; "`. |
+| 9 | **Stateful model test**: the parser's token-navigation API (`next_token`, `prev_token`, `peek_token`, `peek_nth_token`) behaves like a cursor over the non-whitespace token list, yielding EOF past the end | `hegel_parser_token_navigation_matches_model` with `#[hegel::state_machine] impl TokenCursor` | Method docs in src/parser/mod.rs ("Return the first non-whitespace token that has not yet been processed or Token::EOF", "Seek back the last one non-whitespace token. Must be called after `next_token()`"). The model is the tokenizer's output filtered of whitespace plus an integer cursor; `prev_token` is gated on its documented precondition. |
+| 10 | The tokenizer never panics on arbitrary input, for 9 dialects and both unescape modes | `hegel_tokenizer_never_panics` (src/tokenizer.rs) | `tokenize_with_location` returns `Result<_, TokenizerError>`; the fuzz target feeds arbitrary strings through the tokenizer via the parser. The `with_unescape` configuration is generated too, not fixed. |
+| 11 | Token spans are internally ordered (`start <= end`) and consecutive tokens are non-overlapping and in source order | `hegel_token_spans_are_ordered_and_nonoverlapping` | `Span` docs: "A span represents a linear portion of the input string (start, end)"; `tokenize_with_location` docs. |
+| 12 | `Span::union` is commutative (including the documented empty-span special case) | `hegel_span_union_commutes` | `union` docs: "Returns the smallest Span that contains both" (a symmetric spec); "If either span is `Span::empty`, the other span is returned". |
+| 13 | For non-empty spans, `Span::union` contains both operands | `hegel_span_union_contains_operands` | Same doc sentence. |
