+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+### Bracket-quoted `Ident` Display does not escape `]`, breaking the AST -> SQL round-trip
+
+**Minimal counterexample (as shrunk by hegel):**
+
+```
+let value = "0";
+let draw_1 = true;      // inject ']'
+let draw_2 = 0;         // ...at position 0, so value = "]0"
+panicked: quoted identifier "SELECT []0]" failed to parse:
+sql parser error: Expected: end of statement, found: 0 at Line: 1, Column: 10
+```
+
+I.e. `Ident::with_quote('[', "]0")` displays as `[]0]`, which does not parse.
+
+**Why it is a library bug, not a test bug:**
+
+- The crate's headline feature is that "the original SQL text can be generated
+  from the AST" (src/lib.rs).
+- The tokenizer *already implements the inverse operation*:
+  `parse_quoted_ident` (src/tokenizer.rs:2364-2385) folds a doubled closing
+  delimiter, so `SELECT [a]]b]` parses to `Ident { value: "a]b", quote_style: Some('[') }`
+  (verified manually). T-SQL's QUOTENAME escapes `]` the same way.
+- But the Display side never produces that escape: re-displaying the AST
+  parsed from `SELECT [a]]b]` yields `SELECT [a]b]`, which no longer parses.
+  So even SQL *produced by the parser itself* fails to round-trip.
+
+**Exact code path at fault:** `impl fmt::Display for Ident`,
+src/ast/mod.rs (line ~388):
+
+```rust
+Some('[') => write!(f, "[{}]", self.value),
+```
+
+— unlike the `"`/`'`/`` ` `` arm above it, it does not pass the value through
+any escaping. `impl fmt::Display for Word` (src/tokenizer.rs:474-475) has the
+same defect for `[`-quoted words. The fix would be to double `]` in the value
+(the tokenizer-side unescape already exists).
+
+The test is kept in place, failing, per the task instructions.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+1. **Full-domain round-trip for `"`/`` ` ``-quoted identifiers and `'`-quoted
+   string literals.** First formulated over arbitrary values; hegel shrank a
+   failure to `Ident { value: "\"\"" }` -> displays as `""""` -> reparses as
+   value `"`. Investigation led to `EscapeQuotedString`
+   (src/ast/value.rs:538-557), whose comment explicitly documents that a
+   value containing an already-doubled quote, or a backslash followed by the
+   quote, is assumed to be *pre-escaped* and passed through verbatim — a
+   deliberate trade-off so that ASTs produced in no-escape parser mode
+   (`ParserOptions::unescape = false`) display correctly. Asserting the
+   round-trip for such values asserts something the code explicitly declines
+   to promise, so the property was narrowed to the documented domain via
+   `strip_preescaped_sequences` (kept as a documented contract limit, not a
+   convenience bound). This is still a lossy corner of the library worth
+   knowing about (an AST value that genuinely contains `""` cannot be
+   serialized faithfully), but the source comment shows it is a known,
+   intentional design decision rather than an undiscovered bug — unlike the
+   bracket case, where no escaping mechanism exists on the Display side at
+   all while the parse side implements one.
+
+2. **Token-span concatenation** (the token texts, concatenated, reproduce the
+   input) was considered but not written: `with_unescape(true)` (the default)
+   rewrites string/identifier token contents, so the property only holds in
+   no-escape mode and only for a subset of tokens — too weak to be worth a
+   test slot. The ordering/non-overlap property (test 11) was kept instead.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+Things that worked well: the workflow ordering (read code -> collect evidence
+-> write properties), the property catalogue (the fuzz-target hint under
+"Generate Configurations, Not Just Payloads" pointed straight at the no-panic
+properties), the "Boundary conjunctions need help" paragraph (directly
+responsible for finding the bracket bug: at 100 uniform cases the test passed;
+after boosting injection of the closing delimiter it failed instantly), and
+gotcha 10 (canary check that cases are really generated — the first all-green
+run in 0.1s looked too good to trust, and the canary proved generation was
+fine).
+
+Friction points, in rough order of importance:
+
+1. **Recursive composite generators are not covered.** A grammar generator
+   for SQL expressions/statements is naturally recursive, but
+   `#[hegel::composite]` cannot call itself: the opaque return type refers to
+   itself and the compiler dies with a baffling error at the *call site*:
+   `error[E0283]: type annotations needed: cannot satisfy 'impl Fn(TestCase)
+   -> std::string::String: std::marker::Send'`. Nothing in reference.md
+   connects that error to recursion. The working pattern (found by trial and
+   error) is a plain function returning a boxed generator that recurses
+   through the box:
+   ```rust
+   fn sql_expr(depth: u32) -> generators::BoxedGenerator<'static, String> {
+       compose!(|tc| { ... tc.draw(sql_expr(depth - 1)) ... }).boxed()
+   }
+   ```
+   The reference should show this pattern (parsers/trees are a very common
+   test subject).
+
+2. **`BoxedGenerator` has a lifetime parameter.** The only mention in
+   reference.md is `let gen: BoxedGenerator<i32> = ...` (no lifetime, and in
+   inferred position so it compiles there). In return position it fails with
+   `error[E0106]: missing lifetime specifier`. Should be documented as
+   `BoxedGenerator<'static, T>` for the common case.
+
+3. **`compose!` rejects `move`.** The macro is `(|$tc:ident| { ... })` and
+   already inserts `move` internally, but writing the natural
+   `compose!(move |tc| { ... })` (needed-looking, since the closure captures
+   locals) fails with `error: no rules expected keyword 'move'`. Worth one
+   sentence in the macro's section: "do not write `move`; captures are moved
+   automatically".
+
+4. **Macro-name ambiguity with `pretty_assertions`.** This test file
+   glob-imports `pretty_assertions::assert_eq` at the top level; inside a
+   nested `mod` with `use super::*`, every `assert_eq!` fails with
+   `error[E0659]: 'assert_eq' is ambiguous` (glob import vs std prelude).
+   Fixed by an explicit `use pretty_assertions::assert_eq;` inside the
+   module. A gotcha entry would help — the skill's own advice to add tests to
+   existing test files makes hitting this likely in any crate that uses
+   `pretty_assertions` (as sqlparser does).
+
+5. **Generators are consumed by `draw`, so they can't be bound and reused.**
+   Writing `let coord = one_of!(...); ... tc.draw(coord); tc.draw(coord)` is
+   a move error. Obvious in hindsight (`draw(gen: impl Generator<T>)` takes
+   by value), but the reference never says generators are single-use values;
+   a note plus the "wrap it in a closure that builds a fresh generator"
+   idiom would save a compile cycle.
+
+6. **Stateful testing section was otherwise accurate.** `#[hegel::state_machine]`,
+   rules with `tc.assume` preconditions, invariants, and
+   `hegel::stateful::run(machine, tc)` all worked exactly as documented, first
+   try, including drawing inside rules. The note that `run` consumes the
+   machine was useful.
+
+7. **Minor:** the counterexample output labels draws `let value = ...`,
+   `let draw_1 = ...` from variable names/positions — adequate, but it was
+   only understandable because the draws were few; nothing in the docs
+   explains the labeling scheme.
+
+Everything else (setup via `cargo add --dev hegeltest`, the `hegeltest`
+package / `hegel` crate naming, `#[hegel::test]` with `test_cases = N`,
+`from_regex`, `sampled_from`, `one_of!` with `.boxed()` branches, `tc.reject()`
+in a non-`!` context) worked as documented on the first attempt.
